 We evaluate the class-risk quantities on the complete Study~2 test banks, using each bank's own empirical second moment. These are deterministic characterizations of the observed distributions, not held-out scores of training-fitted predictors. At $r=16$, the mean budgets are $\gamma_r=0.98510/0.93528$ for C7/D4. Table~\ref{tab:core-risk-summary}a bounds the mean exact-action risks by $[0.02980,0.05863]$ and $[0.12944,0.24895]$, compared with independent linear optima of $0.02563/0.10321$. The corresponding identity-family risks are $0.76012/0.52932$. Thus the bounds support substantially better prediction than this no-change reference on average, while quantifying a cost relative to unconstrained linear prediction.
 
+\Cref{fig:same-metric-risks} places these class bounds beside both independent optima and the identity reference in every primary condition. It separates two conclusions that a domain mean can obscure: an exact action can improve on the no-change reference, while imposing exact orthogonality and group laws can still cost accuracy relative to independent prediction. The absolute risks vary across models and readouts; the additive costs and their heterogeneity are quantified below rather than inferred from distances on the logarithmic axis.
